om-up.\n\nGiven the parent map, we can build a tree. For each node, compute its own stability as (death_lambda - birth_lambda) where death_lambda is: if node has children, death_lambda = min(children.birth_lambda) maybe? Actually the cluster dies at the lambda where it splits into children. In HDBSCAN, the cluster's lifetime is the lambda range where it exists as a distinct cluster. It is born at its own birth_lambda, and it dies at the lambda where it splits (the birth of its children). That is the death for the cluster. For leaf clusters, the cluster continues to exist until its points exit? But usually leaf clusters exist until the lambda at which all its points become noise, which is the max point_exit_lambda? Or the cluster dies at the lambda where it merges with something else? In our tree, leaves have no children, so they die at the maximum lambda where any point leaves? Actually in HDBSCAN, as lambda increases, leaf clusters eventually disappear as points leave into noise. So death = max point_exit_lambda? Yes.\n\nSo define: For each cluster, compute death_lambda = min(child death?) Not. For internal node, death_lambda = min(birth_lambda of its children) because that's when it splits. That makes sense: the cluster exists from its birth until the birth of its first child, at which point it splits. So the cluster's own stability is death_lambda - birth_lambda. For leaf node (no children), death_lambda = max of its point_exit_lambdas (if list non-empty). If empty, maybe infinity? But we have non-empty lists. For the root, death_lambda = min(birth_lambda of its children) = min(0.5,0.8)=0.5, stability = 0.5-0.2=0.3. For c1: leaf, death = max(0.7,1.0,1.2)=1.2, stability = 1.2-0.5=0.7. For c2: leaf, death = 1.1, stability = 0.3. That matches earlier.\n\nNow for selection: min_cluster_size = 3. So we only consider clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. c2 is excluded. c0 and c1 are candidate. Then we apply the HDBSCAN optimization: starting from leaves, choose whether to select a node or its descendants based on stability. Typically, for each node, compute its own stability. Then compare with sum of stabilities of its selected descendants. If node's stability > sum of selected descendants, then select the node and mark descendants as not selected. Else, keep the descendants selected. This is a standard condensation procedure.\n\nLet's apply to this tree:\n- c2 is a leaf but size <3, so it is not considered at all. So its stability is not used?\n- c1: leaf, size>=3, so it is a candidate. Its stability = 0.7.\n- c0: root, size>=3. Its stability = 0.3. Its direct children are c1 and c2. c2 is below min_cluster_size, so it is not considered a valid cluster. So effectively, c0's only valid child cluster is c1. So we compare c0 stability (0.3) with c1 stability (0.7). Since c0 is less, we select c1 rather than c0. So selected = [c1]. That seems consistent.\n\nThus the output for this fixture would be:\n{\n  \"clusters\": [\n    {\"cluster_id\": \"c0\", \"size\": 7, \"stability\": 0.3},\n    {\"cluster_id\": \"c1\", \"size\": 4, \"stability\": 0.7},\n    {\"cluster_id\": \"c2\", \"size\": 2, \"stability\": 0.3}\n  ],\n  \"selected_cluster_ids\": [\"c1\"]\n}\n\nBut is the stability value a float? Should be number. 0.3 is 0.3, 0.7 is 0.7. Since the output schema says \"type\": \"number\", we can output floats.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) to compute this. The case has fields: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of dicts), parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent or null). We need to compute per cluster: size (already in input), cluster_id, and compute stability as described. Then determine selected_cluster_ids.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n1. Parse input: clusters list, parent dict, min_cluster_size.\n2. Build a dict from cluster_id to cluster info (including birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas, size, cluster_id). Also build adjacency: for each cluster, its children list.\n3. For each cluster, determine death_lambda:\n   - If the cluster has children (i.e., any other cluster with parent == this cluster_id), then death_lambda = min(birth_lambda of its children). Because the cluster splits at the birth of its first child.\n   - Else (leaf), death_lambda = max(point_exit_lambdas) if point_exit_lambdas non-empty. If empty, set death_lambda = something? Not in fixture, but could assume 0 or infinity? Might not occur.\n4. Compute stability = death_lambda - birth_lambda.\n5. Build tree and compute selected clusters using condensation with min_cluster_size filter:\n   - We'll do a post-order traversal of the tree (starting from leaves). For each node, we will compute the \"best selection\" of clusters within its subtree.\n   - For leaf nodes: if size >= min_cluster_size, then its own stability is considered; otherwise it is not a valid cluster. For internal nodes: consider its own stability if size>=min_cluster_size, and the sum of stabilities from selected subtrees of its children. Compare and choose the higher one. Then mark which clusters are selected.\n   - Implementation: recursive function that returns (selected_stability_sum, selected_ids) for the subtree. The selection criteria: for node, compute own_stability (if size>=min_cluster_size, else 0). Compute children_sum = sum of selected_stability_sum from each child (which already includes the best selection for child's subtree). If own_stability > children_sum, then selection for this subtree is (own_stability, [node.cluster_id]) plus children selected? Actually careful: In HDBSCAN, when you select the node, you exclude all descendants. So the subtree selection is just the node itself. When you select children, you keep their selected clusters and the node is not selected. So we need to propagate the set of selected IDs. So for a node:\n      - Compute best_stability and best_ids:\n        option1: own_stability (if size>=min_cluster_size) and no descendants from children. So stability1 = own_stability, ids1 = [node.cluster_id].\n        option2: if node has valid children (size>=min_cluster_size?) but children may have been filtered. Actually children's best selection already accounts for min_cluster_size within their subtrees. So children_sum = sum(child_best_stability). Then stability2 = children_sum, ids2 = union of child_best_ids.\n        If own_stability > children_sum, choose option1, else option2. (If equal can go either way, but typical implementation chooses children? Or node? Usually ties are resolved by selecting the node? Not sure. Use > for node, else children.)\n        But we also need to consider that children may be skipped if they are too small? The child's own best selection already handles min_cluster_size. So fine.\n   - The root's best_ids give the selected_cluster_ids.\n   - Additionally, we need to output all clusters with their computed stability (regardless of selection). The stability is as computed in step 4.\n\nEdge cases: If a cluster has no point_exit_lambdas (empty list) and is leaf, death_lambda undefined. Possibly treat death_lambda as infinity? But then stability infinite. That's unlikely. Could treat as max of all lambdas from entire dataset? Or just set to some high value. Since not in fixture, we can assume non-empty.\n\nComplexities: The recursion may need to handle multiple children.\n\nNow, we need to produce code that matches the spec. The function solve(case) receives the payload object directly. It should return a dict with keys \"clusters\" and \"selected_cluster_ids\".\n\nLet's write the code. Use standard Python: no imports needed.\n\nImplementation:\n- Extract min_cluster_size, clusters list, parent dict.\n- Build dict cluster_data: key cluster_id, value dict with birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas, size, children list.\n- Build children lists from parent.\n- Compute death_lambda and stability for each:\n   For each cluster:\n      if children: death = min(cluster_data[child]['birth_lambda'] for child in children)\n      else: death = max(cluster_data[cid]['point_exit_lambdas']) (assuming non-empty).\n      stability = death - birth\n- Build output clusters list with cluster_id, size, stability (size from input, stability computed).\n- Build top-level nodes (those with parent None).\n- Define recursive function that takes node cluster_id and returns (total_stability, selected_ids) for that subtree, considering min_cluster_size. Use the computed stability for own_stability. For children, call on each child.\n\n- Then call for each root? But parent map indicates only one root (c0 has parent null). If multiple roots, we need to combine? Usually there is one root (the whole space). But handle multiple if parent null exists for multiple? Could happen if there are separate forests. Then we need to run selection per root and union results? But the algorithm typically expects a single hierarchy. We'll assume one root. But to be safe, find all roots (parent is None or not in parent dict? Actually parent keys are cluster_ids, so a root is a cluster_id that either doesn't appear as a value in parent, or whose parent is None. But we have explicit parent dict for each cluster. So iterate and find those with parent None.\n\n- For each root, compute best. Since clusters are disjoint trees, we can select from each independently and combine selected IDs.\n\n- Note: min_cluster_size also applies to root? Yes, if root size < min_cluster_size, its own stability not considered, but its children may still be selected? In that case, root's own_stability would be 0. Then children_sum might be positive, so we select children. That is fine.\n\nNow code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters = case['clusters']\n    parent = case['parent']\n    \n    # Build lookup\n    data = {}\n    for c in clusters:\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\n        data[cid] = {\n            'birth': c['birth_lambda'],\n            'exit': c['point_exit_lambdas'],\n            'size': c['size'],\n            'children': []\n        }\n    # Populate children\n    for cid, pid in parent.items():\n        if pid is not None:\n            data[pid]['children'].append(cid)\n    \n    # Compute stability for each\n    cluster_stability = {}\n    for cid, d in data.items():\n        if d['children']:\n            # internal node: death = min of children's birth\n            death = min(data[child]['birth'] for child in d['children'])\n        else:\n            # leaf: death = max of point_exit_lambdas\n            # assume non-empty\n            death = max(d['exit'])\n        stability = death - d['birth']\n        cluster_stability[cid] = stability\n    \n    # Build output clusters list\n    out_clusters = []\n    for cid, d in data.items():\n        out_clusters.append({\n            'cluster_id': cid,\n            'size': d['size'],\n            'stability': cluster_stability[cid]\n        })\n    \n    # Build roots: clusters with parent None\n    roots = [cid for cid in parent if parent[cid] is None]\n    # If no explicit root? parent dict might have all keys? In the fixture it has all clusters. So fine.\n    # Recursive selection\n    def best_subtree(cid):\n        d = data[cid]\n        own_stab = cluster_stability[cid] if d['size'] >= min_size else 0\n        # compute children sum and ids\n        children_sum = 0\n        children_ids = []\n        for child in d['children']:\n            child_stab, child_ids = best_subtree(child)\n            children_sum += child_stab\n            children_ids.extend(child_ids)\n        if own_stab > children_sum:\n            return own_stab, [cid]\n        else:\n            return children_sum, children_ids\n    \n    selected_ids = []\n    for root in roots:\n        _, ids = best_subtree(root)\n        selected_ids.extend(ids)\n    \n    # Ensure no duplicate? but shouldn't be.\n    return {\n        'clusters': out_clusters,\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_ids\n    }\n\nEdge cases: What if a leaf has empty point_exit_lambdas? Then max w